menu "Claude Post power"

config CLAUDEPOST_DEEP_SLEEP
    bool "Sleep between polls (battery mode)"
    default y
    help
      Turn the board into a picture frame that wakes, polls, and goes back to
      sleep without ever powering the panel. Spectra 6 is bistable, so the last
      edition stays on the glass while the chip draws microamps.

      What it buys, on a 4200 mAh cell, assuming two content changes a day:

        always on (today)     1944 mAh/day     2.2 days
        1 min                  150-160         26-28 days
        5 min                   35-41          100-120 days
        15 min                  16-22          190-260 days
        30 min                  11-17          250-380 days
        60 min                   8-14          300-520 days

      The knee is 15-30 minutes; past it, deep-sleep current and the refreshes
      dominate and a longer interval buys progressively less. Two terms in those
      numbers are estimates rather than measurements, which is why they are
      ranges — GET /api/state reports what this board actually does after a day
      on the wall.

      It is ON by default, and what makes that safe is that this switch is not
      what decides. Three runtime gates disable sleep whatever this says: no
      cell fitted, a USB console attached, or no news URL configured. A board on
      a bench satisfies at least one of them permanently — it is on USB, and
      usually has no battery — so it never sleeps, `idf.py monitor` keeps
      working, and this option changes nothing about developing on one. A board
      on a wall satisfies none of them, and sleeps.

      That asymmetry is the whole argument for the default. The alternative is
      an option somebody has to find and turn on before the feature they
      actually wanted exists, and a frame that runs flat in two days until they
      do. Off by default would only have protected the case the gates already
      protect.

      Read "Getting back in" in docs/specs/2026-08-17-deep-sleep-design.md
      before hanging one up, though: a sleeping board is a board you cannot
      reach between wakes, and the escape hatch is KEY2 held for five seconds.
      That is worth knowing before you need it.

config CLAUDEPOST_SLEEP_SECONDS
    int "Sleep between polls (seconds)"
    default 900
    range 60 86400
    help
      The interval when nothing has changed. Only consulted when deep sleep is
      enabled and permitted.

      This is the build-time floor of three layers: the captive portal writes an
      override into NVS, and POST /api/sleep changes it from a phone at runtime.
      Taking a frame off a wall and finding a USB-C cable in order to change a
      polling interval is the thing that gets resented within a month, so the
      other two exist and this one is only the default.

      15 minutes is the knee of the curve in the table above. Shorter buys
      freshness the reader almost never sees — this is a newspaper — and costs
      the cell steeply below about five minutes.

config CLAUDEPOST_AWAKE_WINDOW_SECONDS
    int "How long a button wake stays reachable (seconds)"
    default 120
    range 10 3600
    help
      A timer wake sleeps again in about three seconds. A BUTTON wake means a
      person is standing in front of the frame, so the board stays up this long
      with the companion app's HTTP server and mDNS running, then sleeps.

      Without it the companion app is effectively dead on a sleeping board: it
      cannot discover, connect and issue a request inside a three-second window.
      About 5.7 mAh per press, incurred only when somebody presses — the
      standing cost is zero. Any command or button press restarts the window, so
      a user mid-interaction is not cut off.

      Half of that figure is this window and half is the refresh beside it: a
      button wake takes the full path, RAM did not survive the sleep, so the
      first fetch never matches the demo page in memory and the sheet is
      reprinted even though the glass already holds it. 2.8 mAh of window, 2.3
      of refresh, ~0.6 of being awake to do it.

endmenu
